  "notes": "⛔ **This is the Bay half of the archetype, and the parameter that says so is `drums.bass808`, which the g-funk genre sets to `null`.** The research states the two-bass-layer split as the mobb signature — \"sub-bass is 'the star' — ultra-deep, tuned for car resonance, plus a separate rubbery mid-bass funk line\" — and the engine has exactly one way to say that: an 808 lane with `role: \"counter_riff\"` (so it is a tuned pedal and *not* the bass part) riding the kick at `lockTo808` 0.9, under a separate `bassline` that plays the rubbery funk line. With `role: \"bassline\"` the melodic bass would not generate at all, which would be the wrong half. `slideProb` is 0.06 so the sub stays a pedal — this is a long sine tail on the kick, not a trap glide. The rest is mobb rather than LA: slower than the genre (84–96), crisp claps/rimshots on 2 and 4, steady swung 16th hats, and \"simple looping 2–4 bar motifs, sparser than LA g-funk and less dependent on P-Funk interpolation\", hence a low `densityPerBar` against the genre's whining worm lead, which is inherited unchanged. Documented progressions `i–VI–VII` and `i–iv–VII` are authored as-is. ⚠ `medium` on the research's high-medium. The INFERRED parameter is the vocal one that cannot be modelled at all: the research calls his rhythmic displacement — triplet and sextuplet bursts against straight 16ths, 2 to 9 syllables a beat — the load-bearing parameter of the entry, and there is no vocal generator, so it survives only as the melody's wide `octaveJumpProb` and leap share.",
